Primary Dysmenorrhea
Common Infections and Afflictions
Suprapubic cramping with menses, Normal pelvic examination, Onset shortly after menarche

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-038
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, agonizing lower abdominal cramping and vomiting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an adolescent/young adult patient experiencing acute, agonizing suprapubic cramping, intractable vomiting, and severe generalized malaise preventing any oral fluid intake. The intensity of the pain, radiating to the back and thighs, combined with active vomiting, generates immense distress and raises valid clinical concern for acute surgical pathology (e.g., ruptured ectopic pregnancy, acute appendicitis, or ovarian torsion). Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform an acute abdominal and pelvic evaluation, definitively rule out an acute abdomen, and administer immediate, potent parenteral or mucosal analgesia/antiemetics. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of missing a life-threatening surgical emergency, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: The cramps are so violent I can't stand up, and I can't keep a drop of water down because I keep vomiting from the pain.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 6 hours ago, precisely coinciding with the onset of the patient's menstrual flow. The patient describes severe, cramping lower abdominal pain radiating intensely to the lower back and upper thighs. This is accompanied by debilitating nausea, episodic vomiting, fatigue, and general malaise. Altered bowel habits (mild diarrhea) are also reported. The patient notes the onset of these severe cyclical symptoms began approximately twelve months after menarche, but states this current episode is exceptionally severe. Denies fever, abnormal vaginal discharge, dyspareunia, or urinary symptoms.
Functional Status: Currently bed-bound, curled in a fetal position due to extreme pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to excruciating pain and inability to retain fluids.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, but visibly pale and sweating. Acutely distressed, actively wincing and guarding the lower abdomen.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A normal transpelvic ultrasound ruling out secondary causes like endometriosis or fibroids would assist in confirming the diagnosis chronically, but is not acutely indicated for this triage. Negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test (beta-hCG) is highly recommended.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized, deep tenderness strictly in the suprapubic region. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of focal right lower quadrant pain (McBurney's point tenderness), guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Bilateral renal angles are non-tender. A non-invasive external pelvic examination (if structurally indicated) lacks palpable adnexal masses or structural abnormalities. Visual inspection confirms normal menstrual bleeding.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin beside the bed contains small amounts of clear fluid/bile.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient vomited the Paracetamol 500mg taken an hour ago.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Primary Dysmenorrhea - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Ectopic Pregnancy - Clinically less likely given the explicit history of cyclical pain and the lack of peritoneal signs (rebound/guarding) or shock, but must strictly be ruled out with a pregnancy test in any female of childbearing age presenting with acute lower abdominal pain.
Acute Appendicitis - Definitively ruled out by the lack of migratory pain to the right lower quadrant, absence of fever, and negative localized peritoneal signs.
Endometriosis (Secondary Dysmenorrhea) - Unlikely as the sole primary diagnosis given the onset closely following menarche and the classic immediate relief typically provided by NSAIDs (once absorbed), though it remains a long-term differential if symptoms become entirely refractory.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of agonizing lower abdominal pain and vomiting required an urgent home evaluation to definitively exclude acute surgical intra-abdominal or pelvic pathology, such as an ectopic pregnancy or appendicitis. Direct physical examination confirmed a soft abdomen without peritonism, and symptoms strongly localized to a severe functional, prostaglandin-driven primary dysmenorrhea. By establishing a benign clinical diagnosis, providing immediate non-oral antiemetics and potent NSAID therapy, and reassuring the highly distressed patient, an unnecessary and traumatic emergency department transport was successfully avoided. This time-critical assessment and intervention satisfy MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually stat to arrest the vomiting cycle. Once nausea subsided, administered Naproxen 500mg orally stat. Prescribed Naproxen 250mg strictly every 8 hours with food for the next 48-72 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the severe cramping from starting, you must take the anti-inflammatory medication (Naproxen) at the absolute first sign of your period, or even the day before if you are regular, rather than waiting for the pain to become severe. The medication works by completely blocking the production of the chemicals (prostaglandins) that cause the uterus to violently spasm; if you wait until the spasm starts, the chemicals are already in your bloodstream, and the medication is far less effective.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Application of a heating pad to the lower abdomen or lower back.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 4-6 weeks to discuss long-term management options, including hormonal contraception to suppress ovulation and reduce prostaglandin production, and to organize a routine pelvic ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the pain suddenly becomes rigidly sharp and constant on one specific side, if you develop a high fever, or if you begin bleeding heavily enough to soak through a large pad every hour.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; secondary causes of severe period pain must be investigated if standard medications completely fail.
Clinical Course: The extreme pain and nausea will begin to subside within 1-2 hours of the medications taking effect. The severe cramping typically lasts only for the first one to three days of the menstrual cycle.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you become dizzy and feel faint upon standing, which could indicate severe internal bleeding or severe dehydration.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your body is releasing massive amounts of a chemical that forces the uterus to cramp and shed its lining. In some people, this chemical also enters the bloodstream, causing the nausea and diarrhea. The prescribed anti-inflammatory strictly blocks the production of this exact chemical, addressing both the pain and the vomiting simultaneously.
Actionable Steps: Apply a hot water bottle to your lower abdomen; the heat acts directly on the muscles to dilate blood vessels and physically relax the severe uterine spasms. Avoid caffeine during the first 48 hours of your cycle, as it constricts blood vessels and worsens cramping.

SMS INFORMATION
Severe Period Pain (Primary Dysmenorrhea). Sublingual anti-nausea given. Start strong anti-inflammatories (Naproxen) immediately. Apply a heat pack.
SMS EDUCATION
Take Naproxen with food. Start medication before pain becomes severe next month. Go to ED immediately if pain becomes sharp on one side or bleeding becomes excessively heavy.

